window.OSAP_FT=window.OSAP_FT||{};window.OSAP_FT["3e"]={"https://theconversation.com/view-from-the-hill-albanese-says-he-wants-chalmers-there-for-the-long-haul-296268":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:16Z","sha":"4a79ed8676fb3337ede6ddda989a2b73fdc27bb171a98719ff105ad91f5990d7","read":"https://theconversation.com/view-from-the-hill-albanese-says-he-wants-chalmers-there-for-the-long-haul-296268","bytes":173739,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"View from The Hill: Albanese says he wants Chalmers there for the long haul","by":[{"n":"Michelle Grattan","r":"Professorial Fellow, University of Canberra"}],"pub":"2026-11-10T08:32:37Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["In politics, rumour feeds on rumour, and the line between rumour and reality can become increasingly blurred. At the extreme, rumour can in itself help change reality."]},{"k":"p","s":["Treasurer Jim Chalmers, struggling with a mass of economic problems, is presently the centre of the Canberra rumour mill – about his relationship with the prime minister, about colleagues’ allegedly undermining him, even about suggestions he could be shifted out of the Treasury portfolio."]},{"k":"p","s":["Just before he jetted off on Tuesday to the COP climate conference in Turkey, Albanese was asked at a news conference: “Is it your preference that Jim Chalmers remains treasurer through to the next election?”"]},{"k":"p","s":["His answer was a flat “yes”."]},{"k":"p","s":["In another question he was asked: “How concerned are you about the apparent undermining of your treasurer from within government?”"]},{"k":"p","s":["He replied: “The treasurer’s doing a great job. This is a treasurer [who] has produced a couple of budget surpluses, lower deficits, that’s producing economic reform, and we’re continuing to work through with the mid-year economic forecast just in – it’ll come in next month. We’re continuing to work that through. I work closely with the treasurer.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["The answers were less important than the questions. As we know from various fronts, Albanese – who has the sole say over who is his treasurer – can always have a “change of position”."]},{"k":"p","s":["But that the questions were asked reflected that the performance and future of Chalmers, who is under fierce public criticism in sections of the press and seeming private attack from leakers in Labor, has become the object of something of a media feeding frenzy."]},{"k":"p","s":["Once this happens, ministers and other colleagues inevitably find themselves confronted by questions on the matter – and it’s easy to stumble."]},{"k":"p","s":["Thus Environment Minister Murray Watt was asked this week whether he’d like to see an end to the undermining and leaking against Chalmers."]},{"k":"p","s":["He replied: “Yes, I would” ."]},{"k":"p","s":["“As a member of a government, I take seriously the responsibility to work together, to support government policies, and also to get out there and support each other in what, at times, are very difficult times.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["Oops. Fine sentiment. Wrong answer. Watt had confirmed the leaking."]},{"k":"p","s":["He went further. “It’s pretty obvious that there are all sorts of people making all sorts of comments.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["Later, Watt was apparently surprised at the attention to his remarks."]},{"k":"p","s":["Meanwhile by Tuesday cabinet secretary Andrew Charlton, a favourite of the prime minister and seen as destined eventually for higher things, was resorting to the textbook answer when fending off questioning about his ambitions. “I’m very focused on my job.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["Chalmers can do little but also be very focused on his job which, if anything, is only going to get tougher in coming months. And trust Albanese won’t have one of his “changes of position”."]}],"chars":2820},"https://theconversation.com/from-ancient-asia-to-the-ai-age-has-chinas-beauty-stratagem-found-a-new-victim-in-nvidias-jensen-huang-296393":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:30Z","sha":"c49e1d5364f3718c968a3c5fbc108618f7cd8ca3a3a1b549d6b4721a5c990dec","read":"https://theconversation.com/from-ancient-asia-to-the-ai-age-has-chinas-beauty-stratagem-found-a-new-victim-in-nvidias-jensen-huang-296393","bytes":187628,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"From ancient Asia to the AI age: Has China’s ‘beauty stratagem’ found a new victim in Nvidia’s Jensen Huang?","by":[{"n":"Linggong Kong","r":"Ph.D. Candidate in Political Science, Auburn University"}],"pub":"2026-11-12T20:25:32Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Extramarital activities have ",["led to the resignation of","https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/11/technology/jensen-huang-nvidia-affair.html"]," one of America’s most influential tech bosses, Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang. The saga has also raised the specter of an age-old spying tactic: the “honey trap.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["Huang stepped down following ",["media reports","https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/08/technology/nvidia-investigation-jensen-huang-china.html"]," of an extramarital relationship with Alice Huang, a Singapore-based executive ",["suspected of having ties to Chinese intelligence agencies","https://thehill.com/policy/international/6212857-alice-huang-spy-allegations-nvidia-ceo-relationship/"],". She previously worked for Megaspeed, a cloud computing company ",["under U.S. scrutiny","https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/12/nvidia-megaspeed-alice-huang-jensen-.html"]," for allegedly helping China obtain restricted AI chips."]},{"k":"p","s":["Jensen Huang ",["acknowledged an affair","https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/a-message-to-our-company"],". And amid reports of a second ",["with a company executive who reports to him","https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/11/technology/jensen-huang-nvidia-affair.html"],", he tendered his resignation to the Nvidia board on Nov. 12, 2026."]},{"k":"p","s":["The sensitivity of Huang’s work – AI is increasingly seen as ",["central to national security","https://www.belfercenter.org/publication/artificial-intelligence-and-national-security"]," – has turned a company matter into an international issue. China’s Foreign Ministry has ",["denied the espionage allegations","https://x.com/CGTNEurope/status/2119783595040702490"]," and ",["accused the U.S.","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/12/china-says-us-fabricating-nvidia-spy-claims-to-justify-tech-blockade"]," of “fabricating so-called spy narratives and ⁠⁠⁠⁠using them as a pretext for imposing ⁠⁠⁠⁠a technological blockade.” Nvidia says ",["it has no reason to believe","https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/statement-from-nvidia-board-of-directors"]," company secrets have been leaked."]},{"k":"p","s":["Yet part of what makes the accusations so believable is ",["history is dotted","https://engelsbergideas.com/notebook/a-short-history-of-the-honey-trap/"]," with examples of men being distracted, compromised or spied on by attractive men and women deployed for that purpose."]},{"k":"p","s":["Indeed, in China it recalls an ancient political tactic known as “meiren ji” or the “ ",["beauty stratagem","https://doi.org/10.17265/2159-5542/2025.05.001"],".”"]},{"k":"p","s":["As a ",["scholar specializing in U.S.-China relations","https://scholar.google.com/citations?hl=zh-CN&user=SfkpAykAAAAJ"],", I believe that regardless of the particulars of the Huang case, the political logic behind the beauty stratagem – using personal relationships to obtain information, influence decisions and weaken opponents – remains relevant today. As such, it would be no surprise if the tactic was being used amid the growing U.S.-China competition over AI and advanced semiconductors."]},{"k":"h","s":["The beauty stratagem of Ancient China"]},{"k":"p","s":["“Meiren ji” is one of “ ",["Thirty-Six Stratagems","https://www.forbes.com/sites/daviatemin/2017/01/02/ancient-wisdom-for-the-new-year-the-36-chinese-stratagems-for-psychological-warfare-in-business-politics-war/"],",” a Chinese military text of uncertain origin but generally believed to have derived from oral and written histories from the Warring States period — 475–221 BCE. Its 31st stratagem states that personal relationships can be used to influence political opponents and recommends exploiting an adversary’s personal desires and emotional vulnerabilities to weaken their judgment and undermine their leadership."]},{"k":"p","s":["The “Thirty-Six Stratagems” is both a playbook for warfare and a reflection on past experience."]},{"k":"p","s":["Indeed, one of China’s most famous beauty stratagem stories dates back more than 2,000 years to the ",["Spring and Autumn period","https://www.britannica.com/event/Spring-and-Autumn-Period"]," of Chinese history."]},{"k":"p","s":["In the fifth century BCE, King Goujian of Yue, in modern-day southern China, was defeated by King Fuchai of Wu, its northern neighbor, and forced to submit. But Goujian secretly rebuilt his strength and prepared for revenge."]},{"k":"p","s":["According to the ancient historical text “ ",["Wu Yue Chun Qiu","https://cornellpress.cornell.edu/book/9781501754340/spring-and-autumn-annals-of-wu-and-yue/#bookTabs=1"],",” Yue sent two beautiful women – Xi Shi and Zheng Dan – to Fuchai, hoping he would become distracted by pleasure and neglect state affairs."]},{"k":"p","s":["The plan reportedly worked. Fuchai, seemingly enchanted by the beautiful women, gradually ignored Yue’s threat. Meanwhile, Goujian prepared for war and in 473 BCE Yue conquered Wu."]},{"k":"p","s":["Xi Shi’s actual role in Wu’s downfall remains unclear. Wu’s defeat ",["involved poor political decisions","https://www.chiculture.org.hk/sc/china-five-thousand-years/3946?"]," and Yue’s military recovery."]},{"k":"p","s":["Still, the story illustrated an important idea in Chinese political strategy: exploiting an opponent’s personal weaknesses can sometimes be more effective than confronting him directly."]},{"k":"p","s":["A similar tactic appears in the 14th-century novel “ ",["Romance of the Three Kingdoms","https://threekingdoms.com/"],",” considered one of the classics of Chinese literature."]},{"k":"p","s":["It tells the story of Dong Zhuo, a powerful warlord who controlled the imperial court during the late Eastern Han Dynasty. To remove him, Wang Yun, a senior court official loyal to the Han Dynasty promised a beautiful young woman, named Diaochan, in marriage to Dong Zhuo’s military commander. He then offered the same woman to Dong Zhuo."]},{"k":"p","s":["The scheme fueled jealousy and distrust between the two men, eventually enabling Wang Yun to persuade Dong Zhuo’s underling to kill him. Wang Yun then briefly took charge of the imperial court on behalf of the Han emperor."]},{"k":"h","s":["The ‘honey trap’ of modern spyware"]},{"k":"p","s":["In modern intelligence operations, using romantic or sexual relationships to approach targets, obtain sensitive information or exert influence is commonly called a “ ",["honey trap","https://thecsrjournal.in/honey-trapping-spy-tactic-threatening-national-security"],".” It is, moreover, certainly not unique to China. Western and Soviet intelligence agencies used intimate relationships for espionage frequently ",["during the Cold War","https://foreignpolicy.com/2010/03/12/the-history-of-the-honey-trap/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Modern China also has documented cases. One example involves French diplomat Bernard Boursicot and the ",["Chinese opera performer Shi Pei Pu","https://content.time.com/time/specials/packages/article/0,28804,1946375_1946448_1948269,00.html"],". The pair met in Beijing in 1964 and maintained a long-term intimate relationship. Throughout, Boursicot was providing French diplomatic documents to China. The spying was eventually exposed and both involved were prosecuted for espionage in France in the 1980s."]},{"k":"p","s":["A more recent example involves ",["former U.S. Rep. Eric Swalwell and Christine Fang","https://abcnews4.com/news/nation-world/swalwell-and-fang-fang-declassified-fbi-files-reveal-physical-relationship-chinese-intelligence-congressional-office"],", a suspected Chinese intelligence operative. Swalwell acknowledged in 2015 that he had been physically intimate with Fang on several occasions. At the time, Fang helped raise funds for Swalwell’s campaign and helped place an intern in his congressional office."]},{"k":"h","s":["Why Jensen Huang is strategically important"]},{"k":"p","s":["Jensen Huang’s position would make him a prime target for such a “honey trap” operation. His company has become central to the technological competition between the U.S and China. As AI becomes increasingly important to economic development, military capabilities and national security, moreover, Washington has ",["repeatedly tightened restrictions","https://www.csis.org/analysis/insight-us-semiconductor-export-controls-update"]," on advanced AI chip exports to China, hoping to slow its access to computing power."]},{"k":"p","s":["Beijing is ",["investing heavily in domestic semiconductor development","https://www.reuters.com/technology/china-sets-up-475-bln-state-fund-boost-semiconductor-industry-2024-05-27/"],". Yet Chinese companies still ",["need advanced chips","https://www.cfr.org/articles/chinas-ai-chip-deficit-why-huawei-cant-catch-nvidia-and-us-export-controls-should-remain"]," to train AI models and build data centers."]},{"k":"p","s":["From ",["Beijing’s perspective","https://app.www.gov.cn/govdata/gov/201707/20/408540/article.html"],", advanced AI chips matter not only for Chinese technology companies but for future military and technological competition."]},{"k":"p","s":["This puts Nvidia in a particularly important position and helps explain the national security concerns surrounding Huang."]},{"k":"p","s":["However, strategic motives do not prove an intelligence operation occurred. Jensen Huang’s importance does not, in itself, establish that he was targeted by a honey trap or that Alice Huang acted for Chinese intelligence. Those are allegations that still require full investigation."]},{"k":"h","s":["Espionage in the age of AI"]},{"k":"p","s":["Yet it is surely true that in modern politics – just as in ancient China’s rivalries – rivals seek information about opponents, try to influence important decisions and exploit weaknesses to gain strategic advantages."]},{"k":"p","s":["What has changed is who controls important strategic resources."]},{"k":"p","s":["In ancient China, influencing a ruler through personal relationships could affect a country’s political and military decisions. Today, approaching a major technology executive could offer access to sensitive technologies."]},{"k":"p","s":["More than 2,000 years ago, a powerful state could lose its advantage through a ruler’s personal weaknesses. Today, political and technological power are distributed differently."]},{"k":"p","s":["But as long as important decisions depend on individuals, personal relationships may remain a weakness that rivals can exploit."]}],"chars":7314},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/china":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T16:30Z","sha":"c39c91b45d09b24e36ba17ccdd7da5d94e1b5a36cd15e3363e7ce69d0363a523","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/china","bytes":87686,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"China travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-02-25T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-08-27T23:02:55+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["This travel advice covers mainland China. See travel advice for the Special Administrative Regions, ",["Hong Kong","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/hong-kong"]," and ",["Macao","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/macao"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Flash floods along the Nepal-China border"]},{"k":"p","s":["On 26 August 2026, heavy flooding and landslides have caused widespread damage in Gyirong County in Shigatse, Tibet, near the Nepal border. Buildings, roads, other transport and communications infrastructure have been severely damaged."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Chinese authorities and emergency services are responding. The authorities have warned of the risk of further flooding. The situation may change rapidly. Stay away from affected areas where possible. If you are travelling in the region, follow the advice of the Chinese government and local authorities and be alert to the changing situation."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are affected and need assistance from the UK government, call:"]},{"k":"p","s":["From China: +86 (0) 10 5192 4000"]},{"k":"p","s":["From the UK: +44 (0)20 7008 5000"]},{"k":"h","s":["Typhoons"]},{"k":"p","s":["The typhoon season in China normally runs from May to November, affecting southern and eastern coastal regions. Air travel and other forms of transport can be affected. Monitor the progress of approaching storms on the Japan Meteorological Agency and the China Meteorological Administration websites."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["guidance on tropical cyclones","https://www.gov.uk/tropical-cyclones"]," for advice about what to do if you are caught in a typhoon."]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/china/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of China’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in China set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the Chinese Embassy in the UK."]},{"k":"p","s":["Immigration authorities may collect biometric data, including scanned fingerprints and photos, to register your entry into China."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medical screening on arrival or during your time in China"]},{"k":"p","s":["You may need to pass through medical screening on entry into China. This may be body temperature scanning, or more rarely testing such as throat or nasal swabs, blood tests or medical examinations. You may be refused entry into China if you do not comply."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter China, your passport must have an ‘expiry date’ at least 6 months after the date you arrive and 2 blank pages for visas and stamping."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you renew your passport while you are in China, contact your local exit and entry office or call the national immigration service hotline on 12367, before or immediately upon receipt."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Chinese-British dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["China does not recognise dual nationality. If you were born in China to a Chinese national parent, you will be:"]},{"k":"li","s":["considered by the Chinese authorities to have Chinese nationality"]},{"k":"li","s":["treated as a Chinese citizen, even if you used a British passport to enter China"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you enter China on a Chinese passport or identity card, the British Embassy will not be able to offer you help. If you have formally renounced Chinese citizenship, you should carry clear evidence that you have done so. See ",["guidance on nationality in China","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nationality-in-china"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re a former Chinese citizen, Chinese authorities may:"]},{"k":"li","s":["treat you as a Chinese citizen"]},{"k":"li","s":["refuse you access to UK consular services"]},{"k":"p","s":["This can happen even if you entered China on a UK or other foreign passport and you:"]},{"k":"li","s":["haven’t renounced your citizenship according to Chinese law"]},{"k":"li","s":["haven’t formally advised the Chinese authorities of your UK citizenship"]},{"k":"li","s":["continue to maintain a Chinese passport or household registration"]},{"k":"p","s":["Authorities may not allow certain categories of Chinese citizens, such as state officials, to renounce their Chinese nationality under Chinese law."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should get professional legal advice if you’re unsure of your citizenship status under Chinese law."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["Until 31 December 2026, you can enter China visa free for stays of up to 30 days for business, tourism, family or friend visits or transit."]},{"k":"p","s":["This policy applies to all holders of standard British passports whose nationality is listed as ‘British citizen.’ Holders of British Crown Dependency passports whose nationality is listed as ‘British citizen’ are eligible for this policy."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you travel to China for any other reason (this includes paid work, long-term assignments, journalism, or study) or for a stay exceeding 30 days, you must apply for a visa before your trip to China."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you have any doubt about your eligibility, verify the conditions for visa free entry with the Chinese Embassy in London."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you visit China without the appropriate visa or visa free access you will be refused entry."]},{"k":"p","s":["Holders of Emergency Travel Documents are not allowed to enter China without a visa."]},{"k":"p","s":["Visa requirements for ",["Hong Kong","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/hong-kong/entry-requirements"]," and ",["Macao","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/macao/entry-requirements"]," are different."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Applying for a visa"]},{"k":"p","s":["Unless you meet the requirement for visa-free access, you must arrange a visa before travelling to China."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re 14 to 70 years old, you must apply for a visa online and then go in person to a ",["visa for China application centre","https://www.visaforchina.cn/"],". Officials will take a scan of your fingerprints as part of your application. There are visa application centres in London, Manchester, Belfast and Edinburgh. If you’re aged 13 or under, or 71 or over, you do not have to attend in person, but someone will need to submit your application on your behalf."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Chinese Embassy has further information on visa categories and how to apply."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you visit Hong Kong from mainland China and want to return to the mainland, you need a visa that allows you to make a second entry into China."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check your visa details carefully and do not overstay your visa. The authorities carry out regular checks and may fine, detain and deport you."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you want to stay in China longer than 6 months, you may need to get a residence permit."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Teaching visas"]},{"k":"p","s":["Teachers have been detained and deported for working on the wrong visa. It is your responsibility to check you’re working on the correct visa."]},{"k":"h","s":["Working in China"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can only work in China if you have a work visa (Z visa). Tourist and business visas do not allow you to work. You must also hold a valid work permit. The local police regularly carry out checks on companies and schools. If you do not follow Chinese immigration laws, there can be serious penalties, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["imprisonment"]},{"k":"li","s":["fines"]},{"k":"li","s":["deportation"]},{"k":"li","s":["an exit ban, which stops you from leaving China"]},{"k":"li","s":["an exclusion order, which stops you from returning"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you leave the UK, contact the Chinese Embassy in the UK to check Z visa requirements. When submitting your application, and when you receive your work permit, check the details are correct, including the location you’ll be working in. If the details are incorrect – even if your employer or others submitted the application on your behalf – the authorities can detain you, fine you or deport you."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are going to change employer once you’re in China, or change location in China with an existing employer, check with the Chinese authorities whether you need a new visa and work permit before doing so."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter China, you must have a certificate to prove you’ve had a yellow fever vaccination if you’re coming from a ",["country listed as a transmission risk","https://nathnacyfzone.org.uk/factsheet/65/countries-with-risk-of-yellow-fever-transmission"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["For full details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s China guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/49/china#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Registering with the authorities"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must register your place of residence with the local Public Security Bureau within 24 hours of arrival. Chinese authorities enforce this rule with regular spot-checks of foreign nationals’ documentation. If you’re staying in a hotel, they will register you when you check in."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about goods you can take into or out of China. You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"p","s":["This guide also has safety advice for ",["regions of China","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/china/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in China"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist attacks in China cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"p","s":["Although foreigners have not been specifically targeted, attacks may happen in places visited by foreigners. Take particular care during national holidays and at public transport hubs, and always follow the advice of the local authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Risks under Chinese national security laws"]},{"k":"p","s":["Chinese national security laws have broad scope, and you can be arrested without having intended to break the law. See advice in the Laws and cultural differences section below for more information."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political situation"]},{"k":"p","s":["China is ruled by a single political party. Though China is open to foreign visitors, be aware of political and cultural sensitivities."]},{"k":"p","s":["Avoid any demonstrations or large gatherings. The Chinese authorities enforce public order strictly, and you may face arrest, detention and deportation. Foreign journalists have been intimidated, assaulted or detained for trying to report on demonstrations."]},{"k":"p","s":["You may risk becoming a target when there is an anti-foreign feeling or attitude in China. Keep informed of developments and follow the advice of the local authorities. During periods of tension, the authorities may block:"]},{"k":"li","s":["news reporting"]},{"k":"li","s":["access to text-messaging"]},{"k":"li","s":["the internet"]},{"k":"li","s":["international telephone lines"]},{"k":"p","s":["Posting, publishing or supporting political statements critical of China or the Chinese authorities could be viewed as illegal."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Serious crime against foreign nationals is relatively rare, but incidents can happen. Take care of your belongings at major tourist sites and other busy places, particularly where foreign nationals gather."]},{"k":"p","s":["Indiscriminate attacks using vehicles as a weapon, and knife attacks, have occurred in public places including at schools, supermarkets and hospitals. Although rare, there have been isolated violent attacks that have impacted foreign nationals."]},{"k":"p","s":["Penalties for fighting and physical altercations can range from fines to short term detentions to longer-term criminal detentions, regardless of who was initially responsible. Compensation claims by injured parties are commonplace, again regardless of who was initially responsible."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drink spiking and sexual assault"]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not leave drinks unattended or accept drinks from strangers. Personal attacks and sexual assaults are rare, but they can happen, including through drinks being spiked. Women, travelling alone or with female friends, could be at higher risk."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Scams"]},{"k":"p","s":["Beware of scams in popular tourist areas. A common example is the ‘tea tasting’ or ‘massage’ scam. Friendly strangers may invite you to visit a bar, to drink tea or have a massage, and the establishment then demands a large fee. This can be followed by threats, violence or credit card fraud."]},{"k":"p","s":["Due to the prominence of QR code usage in China, travellers are recommended to familiarise themselves with the ",["risks of QR code phishing","https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/blog-post/qr-codes-whats-real-risk"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Commercial disputes"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before entering a commercial contract in China, take legal advice, both in the UK and in China. Contracts agreed in the UK are not always recognised by Chinese courts."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re involved in or connected to a business or civil dispute, the Chinese authorities may impose an exit ban, which means you cannot leave China until the matter is resolved. British nationals have been detained against their will, intimidated and forced to pay money."]},{"k":"p","s":["Violence is rare, but it can be threatened. Report any threats of violence to the Chinese police."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["Always carry your passport with you. Police carry out random checks, especially during periods of heightened security and major sporting or political events. They do not accept printed copies. Failure to produce your ID can lead to a fine or detention."]},{"k":"hs","s":["National security laws"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a risk of detention for alleged breaking of national security laws"]},{"k":"p","s":["China’s authorities have detained foreign nationals who break national security laws. Both ‘national security’ and ‘national interest’ appear regularly in Chinese legislation and have broad scope. You may be detained without having intended to break the law. Activity that happened outside of mainland China – including online activity – could fall under the scope of mainland legislation. There is a risk of arbitrary detention, including of British nationals."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re detained on grounds of national security, you may be held for up to 6 months before formal arrest and denied legal representation before charges are brought."]},{"k":"p","s":["You may be at greater risk of surveillance, harassment or detention while in China if you are currently or have previously been linked to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["foreign governments"]},{"k":"li","s":["media outlets"]},{"k":"li","s":["NGOs"]},{"k":"p","s":["This list is not exhaustive and may change over time. This is particularly relevant when travelling to areas with large ethnic minority populations, including but not limited to Xinjiang. See ",["Regional risks","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/china/regional-risks"]," for more information."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travelling to sensitive areas"]},{"k":"p","s":["All foreign nationals travelling to or around ‘sensitive’ areas might attract more attention from the authorities, including when:"]},{"k":"li","s":["taking photographs"]},{"k":"li","s":["engaging with political groups or charities"]},{"k":"li","s":["engaging with ethnic minority populations"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sensitivities can change over time and may heighten around particular dates or events."]},{"k":"p","s":["There are restricted military areas, where foreigners can be detained, fined or deported if found without prior authorisation. Signs typically indicate where these areas are, but it may not always be clear."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Religious activities and materials"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Chinese authorities formally recognise five religions: Buddhism, Catholicism, Islam, Protestantism and Taoism. Only officially registered religious organisations are permitted to carry out religious activities. Even officially registered organisations must observe restrictions on some religious activities, including preaching and distributing religious materials."]},{"k":"p","s":["Updated ",["religious legislation","https://www.sara.gov.cn/static/content/ywdt/qtyw/2025-04-01/1356550603266883584.html"]," (Chinese language only) entered into force on 1 May 2025, setting out rules that foreigners must abide by when practicing religion in China. The paragraph below summarises some of the key information included in the link, but local legal advice should be sought for full and further detail."]},{"k":"p","s":["Foreigners may participate in religious activity that complies with Chinese law in registered temples, churches, or other approved temporary locations. Foreign nationals may invite Chinese religious professionals to hold baptisms, weddings, funerals, dojos, pujas, and other religious ceremonies for them. Foreigners wanting to hold religious activities or events in approved locations must meet the specific requirements and use the correct application procedures. One of these requirements (Article 10) is that Chinese religious professionals should preside over religious activity. Another (Article 16), is that group religious activities held by foreigners in China are limited to participation by foreigners in China. Collective religious events involving Chinese citizens are prohibited unless officially approved by the Chinese government. There are also rules for foreign religious professionals visiting China to give lectures or sermons, and those bringing in religious materials."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Falun Gong movement and others that the authorities consider as ‘cults’ are banned in China."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Criminal proceedings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Chinese laws and procedures relating to the arrest, detention and trial of criminal suspects are different from in the UK. For example:"]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re detained, only your legal representatives and consular staff from the British Embassy are allowed to visit you until your sentence is passed"]},{"k":"li","s":["there is no trial by jury, the presiding judge or judges will reach a verdict and then pass sentence if they find the accused guilty"]},{"k":"li","s":["if a case goes to trial, the conviction rate is 99%"]},{"k":"li","s":["if an offence does not meet the threshold for criminal proceedings, the police still have the right to sentence someone for up to 15 days administrative detention without trial"]},{"k":"p","s":["The granting of bail is rare, and the criminal process can often last at least a year before a verdict is passed. If bail is granted the accused will not be permitted to leave China."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re suspected of a crime, the Chinese authorities can stop you from leaving China by:"]},{"k":"li","s":["withholding your passport"]},{"k":"li","s":["applying an exit ban"]},{"k":"li","s":["detaining you for up to 37 days without charge"]},{"k":"p","s":["The thresholds for detention and prosecution in China differ from those in Hong Kong."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can be convicted in China of a crime committed against the Chinese State or a Chinese citizen, even if that crime was committed outside of China."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Exit bans"]},{"k":"p","s":["Foreign nationals can be subject to exit bans. Exit bans can:"]},{"k":"li","s":["be put on people involved in commercial or private disputes to stop them from leaving mainland China"]},{"k":"li","s":["relate to investigations into an individual, their family or an employer"]},{"k":"li","s":["be used in criminal and civil matters, including business disputes"]},{"k":"p","s":["An exit ban can last for months or years or until a legal process is complete. You may not be aware you are subject to an exit ban until you try to leave mainland China."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Money"]},{"k":"p","s":["Cashless payments through smartphone apps such as WeChat or Alipay are almost universal."]},{"k":"p","s":["Outside major cities, credit cards are not always accepted and ATMs are limited. It is not possible to exchange Scottish or Northern Irish banknotes."]},{"k":"p","s":["Counterfeit banknotes (especially 100 renminbi notes) are common, including from ATMs. Banks will not replace them. Check notes carefully before accepting them."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs penalties and prison sentences"]},{"k":"p","s":["Possession, trafficking and manufacture of any illegal drugs, including cannabis, are serious offences in China. The Chinese authorities have a zero-tolerance policy and those caught face heavy fines, lengthy prison sentences, or the death penalty."]},{"k":"p","s":["Airports in China have security equipment and procedures in place for scanning and detecting illegal items, including cannabis. This is also used to scan all the baggage of transiting passengers."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Chinese authorities can randomly test foreign nationals for drugs, including on entry to the country. Police can also raid homes as well as nightclubs and bars. If you’re in a bar that’s raided, you will be subject to on-the-spot testing and immigration checks. This may involve:"]},{"k":"li","s":["being kept at the location, or another location, for several hours"]},{"k":"li","s":["giving hair and urine samples"]},{"k":"li","s":["passport and visa checks"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you test positive, the Chinese authorities can prosecute you regardless of where or when you took drugs. Testing positive for drugs, or being found in breach of your visa conditions, can lead to heavy fines, detention and deportation."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Internet access"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Chinese authorities control internet access. Internet access can be limited around key political events and dates. Some services are permanently blocked, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Google"]},{"k":"li","s":["Facebook"]},{"k":"li","s":["YouTube"]},{"k":"li","s":["X"]},{"k":"p","s":["Other websites may sometimes be blocked."]},{"k":"p","s":["China’s cybersecurity laws state that online products and services (for example, VPNs) need to be licensed by the Chinese government. See the Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (in Mandarin). Make sure you stay informed and follow Chinese law."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drone regulations"]},{"k":"p","s":["The import, possession and use of drones and drone components are strictly regulated. Contact the ",["Civil Aviation Administration of China","https://www.caac.gov.cn/English/"]," before travel for the latest rules regarding registration and use."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Building safety"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire protection standards in Chinese accommodation are not always the same as in the UK. You should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check fire precautions, including access to fire exits"]},{"k":"li","s":["make sure your accommodation has a working fire alarm"]},{"k":"li","s":["regularly check fire exits are not blocked"]},{"k":"p","s":["Make sure your accommodation has a working carbon monoxide alarm. People have died of carbon monoxide poisoning due to incorrectly installed gas equipment."]},{"k":"p","s":["Energy UK has ",["advice on how to stay safe and the symptoms to look out for","https://www.energy-uk.org.uk/customers/carbon-monoxide/"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Teaching in China"]},{"k":"p","s":["Thoroughly research the school or university that is hiring you in China and make sure you’re confident they are following the law."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex relationships are legal in China, but public attitudes are less tolerant than in the UK. Showing affection in public may receive negative attention. There’s no Chinese law guaranteeing freedom from discrimination on the grounds of sexual orientation."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"h","s":["Hiking"]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not hike alone in isolated areas, including on the Great Wall, due to unpredictable weather conditions and the chance of injuries. Even when hiking in a group, leave your itinerary, mobile number and expected time of return at your hotel or with a third party."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must have a Chinese driving licence and valid insurance to drive in China. There are harsh penalties for driving without these, including fines and detention."]},{"k":"p","s":["Drive with caution in China. Accidents are common due to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["the poor quality of roads"]},{"k":"li","s":["high volumes of traffic"]},{"k":"li","s":["low driving standards"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re involved in a serious traffic accident, call the police. Do not move your vehicle until they arrive but make sure you and your passengers are in a safe place. If there are injuries, you may be held legally responsible for medical costs. You will also be held responsible if you hit a pedestrian."]},{"k":"p","s":["There are harsh penalties including fines, detention and imprisonment for driving under the influence of alcohol, even at very low levels."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Rail travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must show your passport to buy a ticket and to board a train in China."]},{"k":"p","s":["Trans-Mongolian Express trains (Beijing-Moscow via Ulaanbaatar) are known for smuggling. Petty theft on overnight trains is common. Search your compartment and lock the cabin door before the train leaves."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Sea travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["Mariners should avoid the disputed territory between China and other countries in the East China Sea and the South China Sea. There have been incidents of piracy and armed robbery against ships in the area. ReCAAP Information Sharing Centre has further information about piracy and armed robbery."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taxis"]},{"k":"p","s":["Avoid travelling in unmarked or unmetered taxis, as there have been incidents of sexual assault and robbery against foreign nationals. In marked taxis, make sure someone knows where you are and try to take a note of the taxi’s number."]},{"k":"p","s":["Disputes over taxi fares can quickly escalate. Any physical altercation could lead to detention regardless of who is to blame. Insist on paying the metered fare and ask for a receipt. The receipt should have the taxi number on it."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to ",["prepare for and respond to extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Earthquakes"]},{"k":"p","s":["China is in an active seismic zone and can experience major earthquakes. Learn what to do before, during and after an earthquake from the US Federal Emergency Management Agency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Typhoons"]},{"k":"p","s":["The typhoon season in China normally runs from May to November, affecting southern and eastern coastal regions. Air travel and other forms of transport can be affected. You should monitor the progress of approaching storms on the Japan Meteorological Agency and the China Meteorological Administration websites."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["guidance on tropical cyclones","https://www.gov.uk/tropical-cyclones"]," for advice about what to do if you are caught in a typhoon."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Flooding"]},{"k":"p","s":["Flooding and storms are common between May and November. You should monitor local weather reports and follow the advice and instructions of local authorities, including any evacuation orders."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["This section has safety advice for regions of China. It only covers regions where FCDO has specific advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also read ",["FCDO’s overall travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/china"]," and ",["safety and security advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/china/safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Tibet and the Tibet Autonomous Region (TAR)"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must get a permit to travel to Tibet and the Tibet Autonomous Region (TAR) through a specialist travel agent in China. You must also travel on an organised tour. The Chinese authorities sometimes stop issuing permits without notice or restrict travel to Tibetan Autonomous Prefectures (TAPs) in neighbouring provinces, even if you have a permit. Check with tour operators or travel agents and other media for information."]},{"k":"p","s":["Once in Tibet, avoid large public gatherings. Political and ethnic tensions have historically generated violent protests. Security measures will be tight. Unauthorised gatherings may be dispersed by force. Do not film or take photographs of public security forces or any altercations."]},{"k":"p","s":["Local authorities will react negatively if you’re found carrying letters or packages from Tibetan nationals to be posted in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["You must ask before you take photographs in Buddhist monasteries."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ability of the British Embassy to help British nationals in the TAR is limited."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Xinjiang Uyghur Autonomous Region"]},{"k":"p","s":["Violent unrest in Xinjiang has resulted in deaths. There are allegations that police use lethal force to disperse protests."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Chinese authorities have a significant security presence throughout Xinjiang, both as a visible demonstration of state control and to respond quickly and harshly to any unexpected demonstrations or outbreaks of violence."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been widespread arbitrary arrests and extra-judicial detentions in Xinjiang, mainly affecting the local population, particularly Uyghurs and other ethnic minorities. You may be at increased risk if:"]},{"k":"li","s":["you’re of Turkic descent"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have lived previously in Xinjiang"]},{"k":"li","s":["your appearance or practices are perceived to be Muslim"]},{"k":"li","s":["you publicly participate in Islamic religious practices"]},{"k":"li","s":["you’re a foreign national"]},{"k":"p","s":["Expect airport-style security measures, including passport and security checks, at shopping centres, markets and parks. You may need to give the security forces your phone number, have your photograph taken and explain why you are travelling. Organisations monitoring facilities in Xinjiang are at risk of harassment and detention."]},{"k":"p","s":["Always carry your passport and avoid all protests and large crowds. Do not photograph or film protests, large crowds, security officials or installations, or anything of a military nature. Mosques and other religious sites are also considered ‘sensitive’ by the authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ability of the British Embassy to help British nationals in Xinjiang Uyghur Autonomous Region is limited."]},{"k":"hs","s":["China-Myanmar border"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is continuing violence on the Myanmar side of the China-Myanmar border, which has occasionally spilled over into China."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 120 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for China","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/49/china#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["Altitude sickness is a risk in parts of China. Read more about ",["altitude sickness on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/26/altitude-illness"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in China","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/49/china"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["avian influenza"]},{"k":"li","s":["dengue – present in Guangdong Province and some other parts of China, mainly during the rainy season from May to November"]},{"k":"p","s":["Tap water is generally not safe to drink. Only drink bottled water."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re take prescription medication, make sure you bring enough with you or have access to a supply in China. Certain medicines may not be available and you may be not allowed to bring others into the country. For more information, check with your GP and the Chinese Embassy before travelling."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in China"]},{"k":"p","s":["Healthcare is not free in China and can be very expensive. Make sure you have appropriate travel and medical insurance covering healthcare and medical evacuation and repatriation. For more information, see ",["medical treatment in China","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/medical-treatment-in-china"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a list of ",["doctors and medical facilities in China","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/list-of-hospitals-in-china"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in China"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance: 120"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire: 119"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police: 110"]},{"k":"p","s":["Traffic Police: 122"]},{"k":"p","s":["Maritime Search and Rescue Centre: 12395"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=China"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=China"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=China"]," in China"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in China","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/bereavement-in-china"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in China","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/china-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis,","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"]," such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/china/british-embassy-beijing"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"p","s":["If your passport is lost or stolen, go to the nearest police station or Public Security Bureau and get a report of the incident."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/china/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in China","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-china"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":31562,"change":"\"Updated information on Warnings and insurance\""},"https://theconversation.com/england-needs-120-of-normal-rainfall-to-come-out-of-drought-this-winter-storms-wont-fix-that-yet-295121":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"070fc96555ee1d1cea195a4fc220d106b974faadd774986c8f54d99441e2a15c","read":"https://theconversation.com/england-needs-120-of-normal-rainfall-to-come-out-of-drought-this-winter-storms-wont-fix-that-yet-295121","bytes":181270,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"England needs 120% of normal rainfall to come out of drought this winter – storms won’t fix that yet","by":[{"n":"Hannah Cloke","r":"Regius Professor of Meteorology and Climate Science, University of Reading"}],"pub":"2026-10-29T12:29:56Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Storm Austen and Storm Boelo swept across the UK in quick succession. Rain lashed windows, river levels rose and the clocks went back. It feels like winter has arrived all at once."]},{"k":"p","s":["Yet most of England and ",["Wales","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/crk661074ejo"]," are still in ",["drought","https://theconversation.com/topics/drought-390"],". Parts of Cornwall, Devon, Dorset, Somerset and Sussex are in ",["severe drought","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/government-activates-plan-to-protect-consumers-as-severe-drought-declared-in-parts-of-england"],", the highest level the Environment Agency can declare. South-west and south-east England have had their driest April to September in over 100 years."]},{"k":"p","s":["But how can this be when it is cold, dark and pouring with rain? Drought tightens or loosens its grip over months, not just a few days. It will be the rainy or dry nature of the months ahead that will decide whether the drought ends, or more worryingly, if it stays into next summer."]},{"k":"h","s":["Winter should fill the tank"]},{"k":"p","s":["Summer droughts grab the headlines and public attention. When we have a dry period over the summer it’s ",["everywhere, with brown lawns, hosepipe bans and cracked reservoir beds","https://www.aol.com/articles/devastating-impact-england-drought-pictures-110348000.html"],". But winter is when the UK refills its water stores."]},{"k":"p","s":["Between October and March, with shorter and colder days, less water evaporates and plants drink far less, so rain finally has the chance to refill our reservoirs. Rain can soak deep into the ground to top up groundwater, the water held in chalk and sandstone rocks beneath our feet, like a giant sponge. Groundwater supplies much of the drinking water in southern and eastern England, but it fills slowly, over months and years."]},{"k":"p","s":["Think of it like a bank account. A hot summer is a big splurge with lots of withdrawals. But a dry winter means the deposits needed to repay that spending never arrive. That’s why a dry winter can do more damage to our water supplies than a heatwave."]},{"k":"p","s":["This year the account is badly overdrawn. Southern England has just had its ",["driest April to September","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/water-situation-national-monthly-reports-for-england-2026/water-situation-september-2026-summary#"]," in more than a century, alongside ",["record summer heat","https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/blog/2026/deep-dive-after-a-remarkable-summer-meteorological-autumn-has-arrived"],". The Wimbleball reservoir on Exmoor, which supplies around 400,000 people, was full in March, after an extremely wet winter. Then the weather shifted from wetter than normal to dryer than normal. By October, water ",["levels at Wimbleball","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6d094jgg07eo"]," were down to about 13%."]},{"k":"h","s":["How unusual is a winter drought?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Droughts that carry on into winter are rare in the UK. Usually the autumn rain arrives, soil gets wetter and water stores recover by Christmas. Every instinct suggests that a stormy autumn should fix things."]},{"k":"p","s":["In the 2010 to 2012 drought, ",["two dry winters in a row","https://nora.nerc.ac.uk/id/eprint/503643/"]," left groundwater in south and east England so low that ",["hosepipe bans","https://www.channel4.com/news/hosepipe-ban-to-begin-in-drought-hit-parts-of-uk"]," came in during spring 2012. Then the wettest April on record arrived, and parts of the country swung from drought to flood within weeks."]},{"k":"p","s":["What stands out now is how quickly droughts are stacking up. This is the ",["third drought in England in five years","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/environment-agency-declares-drought-status-in-yorkshire"],". The stores barely have time to recover before the next one begins."]},{"k":"p","s":["England and Wales can be in drought and experience flooding simultaneously. After months of dry weather it takes a lot of rain to wet the ground all the way through. Storms such as Boelo and Austen bring bursts of heavy rain. Drains are overwhelmed and rivers rise quickly, sometimes spilling out onto their floodplains. But the reservoirs and groundwater that supply our taps respond far more slowly, and need lots of water to fill up. So the type of winter rains make a big difference."]},{"k":"p","s":["Floods can hit fast. But the key parts of the water cycle are slow. Rain has to wet the soil all the way through before it can trickle down into the rock, and groundwater can take months to respond. The Environment Agency estimates England needs around ",["120% of normal rainfall","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/government-environment-agency-and-water-companies-step-up-efforts-to-prevent-england-remaining-in-drought-by-next-spring"]," between September and April for every area to come out of drought. We would need half a year with consistently more rain than we would expect at this time of year to break this drought."]},{"k":"p","s":["If rain falls short across the whole winter, then England could start next summer in drought, with even less room for manoeuvre."]},{"k":"h","s":["Will winter droughts become more common?"]},{"k":"p","s":["But climate scientists don’t expect there will be a dry winter every year, either now or in decades ahead as fossil fuel burning continues to heat the planet. Climate projections suggest ",["UK winters will get wetter","https://journals.ametsoc.org/view/journals/clim/33/17/jcliD200089.xml"]," on average. But averages can hide the danger."]},{"k":"p","s":["Summers heated by the burning of fossil fuels ",["dry out the soil","https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/jul/23/climate-crisis-heat-sucking-soils-dry-extreme-drought-europe-analysis"]," and drain reservoirs faster, leaving a bigger hole for winter to fill. Winter rain is increasingly arriving in heavy bursts that are harder to capture and more likely to flood. And natural swings in the weather will still deliver dry winters from time to time. Put those together and the chance of a drought rolling into winter, or across two winters, goes up. This is a trend that will keep getting worse as long as we keep heating the planet."]},{"k":"p","s":["Preparing starts with ",["how we think about water","https://theconversation.com/englands-water-crisis-needs-more-than-just-new-reservoirs-heres-what-will-help-257922"],". It is precious, not endless. Yet we have built a system that lets a wet winter rush away to the sea, then rations what is left through a dry summer."]},{"k":"p","s":["As with other parts of the UK’s water system, we are paying the price now for years of chronic underinvestment. Water companies need to move much faster on fixing leaks, which still ",["waste billions of litres","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/water-resources-2024-2025-analysis-of-the-water-industrys-annual-water-resources-performance/water-resources-2024-to-2025-analysis-of-the-water-industrys-annual-water-resources-performance"]," every day. New ",["reservoirs","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/government-steps-in-to-build-first-major-reservoirs-in-30-years"]," are being planned in England, which will help deliver more storage. But they will take more than a decade to build, cost billions of pounds, and cannot be the whole solution."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["England’s water crisis needs more than just new reservoirs – here’s what will help","https://theconversation.com/englands-water-crisis-needs-more-than-just-new-reservoirs-heres-what-will-help-257922"]]},{"k":"p","s":["There is also a low-tech local answer, that ",["would need regulation","https://www.ciwem.org/news/planet-possible-unlocking-uk-growth-plans-through-water-reuse"]," and a programme of retrofitting to make a big difference. Rainwater harvesting and water reuse in new and existing homes, sustainable drainage in our towns, and encouraging wetlands and floodplains that ",["hold water like a sponge","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nature-based-solutions-environment-agency-position-statement/nature-based-solutions-environment-agency-position-statement"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Changes to our landscape and homes can help prevent ",["both problems","https://www.watermagazine.co.uk/2026/06/29/too-little-water-why-drought-is-the-other-half-of-flood-resilience/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Each of us ",["can help","https://www.southwestwater.co.uk/household/your-services/save-water/come-rain-or-shine"],". Take shorter showers, ask your water company about a meter and and fit a ",["water butt to catch rain","https://www.wessexwater.co.uk/your-water/how-to-install-a-water-butt"]," for the garden. These habits add up."]},{"k":"p","s":["Autumn storms have started to replenish water reserves, but it’s not the finish line. We also need to get ahead of what is likely to happen in years to come."]}],"chars":6012}};
